<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<glossary xml:id="glossary">
<idx>glossary</idx>

<gi>
<title>Complicated Fractions</title>
<p>
    A larger fraction made up of smaller fractions. Also called a complex rational expression
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Extraneous Solution</title>
<p>
    A solution from a rational or root equation that when checked in the original equation, make the equation undefined or non-real.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Factored Form</title>
<p>
    An expression or side of an equation that is completely factored. This leads to one way to solve a quadratic equation.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Factoring</title>
<p>
    The reverse process of multiplication.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Function</title>
<p>
    A function is a relationship that for each input value, there is exactly one output value.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Function Notation</title>
<p>
    Replaces <m>y</m> with <m>f(x)</m> in an equation where <m>x</m> is the input value.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Greatest Common Factor</title>
<p>
    GCF for short. The greatest common factor, is largest combination of numbers and variables that can be factored out from each term of
    a polynomial.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Grouping</title>
<p>
    A technique of factoring where you start with an equal number of groups. Factor the GCF out of each group then factor again to get two, multi-term factors.
</p>
</gi>
<gi>
<title>LCD</title>
<p>
    The least common denominator from a group of rational expressions.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Parabola</title>
<p>
    The graph of a quadratic function.
</p>

</gi>

<gi>
<title>Perfect Square Root</title>
<p>
    A number whose square root is a whole number.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Principal Square Root</title>
<p>
    The positive square root of a number.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Rational Expression</title>
<p>
    A rational expression is a ratio of polynomials.
</p>

</gi>

<gi>
<title>Reciprocal</title>
<p>
    When you swap the numerator and denominator of a fraction.
</p>

</gi>

<gi>
<title>Square Root</title>
<p>
    The inverse process of squaring.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Standard Form</title>
<p>
    A quadratic equation written as <m>y=ax^2 + bx +c</m> or <m>ax^2 + bx  =c = 0</m>.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Vertical Line Test</title>
<p>
    For the graph of a function, every vertical line intersects the graph at most one time.
</p>
</gi>

<gi>
<title>Zero Product Principle</title>
<p>
    If a product of factors equals zero, then anyone of the factors could be zero.
</p>
</gi>
</glossary>